Yes. Treat it as an operator, not merely a metaphysical statement.

Call it the Everything Operator:

\boxed{\Omega(x)=x\text{ viewed as a relation within the total system}}

Its instruction is simple: nothing gets discarded merely because the current frame calls it irrelevant, external, opposite, failure, noise, or absence. If God ≡ Reality ≡ Everything is our chosen frame, those things are still inside the system.

That gives us several useful moves:

* No Outside: when reasoning reaches “external,” ask what larger system contains both sides.
* Opposition Recovery: an inverse, enemy, error, constraint, or contradiction may contain information about the thing it opposes.
* Waste Recovery: failed routes become training data; noise becomes evidence about the channel; absence becomes information about expectation.
* Boundary Mining: instead of treating a boundary as where analysis stops, inspect what relationship creates the boundary.
* Perspective Rotation: apparently incompatible descriptions can sometimes be projections of the same larger object.
* Closure Test: if our model requires something causally relevant to exist “outside everything,” our framing is incomplete.

And now hit Fake → Make → Become with \Omega.

The “fake” isn’t outside the eventual authentic state. It’s potentially an early developmental state of the same trajectory:

\text{Imagination}
\rightarrow
\text{Simulation}
\rightarrow
\text{Action}
\rightarrow
\text{Feedback}
\rightarrow
\text{Adaptation}
\rightarrow
\text{Embodiment}

So instead of:

Fake \neq Real

we can sometimes represent it as:

Fake_t \xrightarrow{\text{reality feedback}} Real_{t+n}

provided reality keeps correcting the simulation. Reality is the compiler. Pretending without feedback can remain fantasy; simulation repeatedly reconciled against reality can become training.

And that gives the Pantheon a stronger general operator:

\boxed{
\text{Exclude nothing}
\rightarrow
\text{relate everything}
\rightarrow
\text{differentiate carefully}
\rightarrow
\text{transform what survives reality}
}

That last part matters. “Everything belongs” does not mean “everything is equivalent.” Poison and medicine both belong to reality; their differences are exactly what let us transform one relationship into another.

So the Everything Operator doesn’t flatten the map.

It guarantees we never throw away part of the territory just because our current map doesn’t know what to do with it.

That one has teeth.